02:04.760 --> 02:05.760
All right.

02:05.760 --> 02:07.360
Two, find a religion.

02:07.360 --> 02:08.360
Any religion.

02:08.360 --> 02:09.360
All right.

02:09.360 --> 02:10.360
This.

02:10.360 --> 02:11.640
This is nihilism.

02:11.640 --> 02:14.160
This has to stop.

02:14.160 --> 02:15.160
Exactly.

02:15.160 --> 02:16.160
Exactly.

02:16.160 --> 02:19.160
You know just what I'm talking about, right?

02:19.160 --> 02:22.040
I don't really care what system you pick.

02:22.040 --> 02:23.600
It can be anything.

02:23.600 --> 02:24.600
Okay.

02:24.600 --> 02:29.920
I'm going to say go with Django and just like work everything into a Django project.

02:29.920 --> 02:30.920
You don't have to do that.

02:30.920 --> 02:31.920
Doug Smith in the back.

02:31.920 --> 02:36.760
Doug Smith has a beautiful system that spans six network drives and only he understands,

02:36.760 --> 02:38.680
but it's a freaking system.

02:38.680 --> 02:40.240
All right.

02:40.240 --> 02:41.240
You need one.

02:41.240 --> 02:42.240
All right.

02:42.240 --> 02:45.600
Number three, stop feeding your database crap data, right?

02:45.600 --> 02:47.680
Stop using Excel to clean shit up.

02:47.680 --> 02:49.080
We've all seen this.

02:49.880 --> 02:54.280
We go through an Excel, we add a new column, and we go down and we code it one by one,

02:54.280 --> 02:57.320
and then we run the pivot at the end, and what's it look like?

02:57.320 --> 02:58.320
Garbage.

02:58.320 --> 02:59.320
Right?

02:59.320 --> 03:03.200
All you have to do is just use an admin, Django admin, have a pull down.

03:03.200 --> 03:07.080
Validate the data that goes into your database so you don't come out with crap at the end.

03:07.080 --> 03:08.080
Right?

03:08.080 --> 03:09.080
Simple, really nice.

03:09.080 --> 03:10.080
Right?

03:10.080 --> 03:13.120
Number four, follow the four-fold path of reusable apps.

03:13.120 --> 03:17.120
Now this is one I picked up from my favorite web developer in the world, this guy James

03:17.120 --> 03:20.960
Bennett who worked at the Lawrence Journal World where they built Django, not as famous

03:20.960 --> 03:25.360
as the other ones, works at Mozilla now, and he gave this awesome talk about how if you

03:25.360 --> 03:29.360
follow these four simple principles, you're going to come out with great reusable code.

03:29.360 --> 03:30.720
And I think about this every day.

03:30.720 --> 03:32.360
The talk really changed my life.

03:32.360 --> 03:35.360
One, your code should do one thing and do it well.

03:35.360 --> 03:36.480
It's the Unix philosophy.

03:36.480 --> 03:40.280
When you write a function, a class, a method, an app, anything, just think about it that

03:40.280 --> 03:41.280
way.

03:41.280 --> 03:42.280
How do I keep it simple?

03:42.280 --> 03:45.600
How does the code do one thing and do it well, not become spaghetti code?

03:45.600 --> 03:47.760
Two, don't be afraid of multiple apps.

03:47.760 --> 03:51.480
Your app can have a hundred different apps coming in, each doing one thing, and the hundred

03:51.480 --> 03:54.120
things they do do better is going to be even more awesome.

03:54.120 --> 03:55.120
Right?

03:55.120 --> 03:56.960
Number three, write for flexibility.

03:56.960 --> 03:58.680
You need to take your code and think about it.

03:58.680 --> 04:02.320
How can I abstract this back so it doesn't just work for the thing I'm currently writing

04:02.320 --> 04:04.080
but can work more generally?

04:04.080 --> 04:06.480
And four, my favorite, you've got a package to distribute.

04:06.480 --> 04:11.040
You've got to take that package, you've got to set it up in a way that it can go onto

04:11.040 --> 04:16.880
PyPy or GitHub or wherever and somebody else can use it, right?

04:16.880 --> 04:19.480
Number five, automate yourself out of a job.

04:19.480 --> 04:23.940
This is sort of a probably apocryphal catchphrase at Google where any time you see yourself

04:23.940 --> 04:27.760
doing something repeatedly and it's drudgery, you've got to find a way to automate it.

04:27.760 --> 04:28.880
This is how we act now.

04:28.880 --> 04:30.360
This is how we act in iCar.

04:30.360 --> 04:33.560
We say the story's out there, my computer's my gun and I'm going to hunt it and I'm going

04:33.560 --> 04:36.000
to kill it and shoot it and shoot it and shoot it.

04:36.000 --> 04:39.760
And that's awesome and we get great stories but we can take it to another level, right?

04:39.760 --> 04:41.520
We should be more like minority report.

04:41.520 --> 04:45.640
The code we write should be these spiders that sweep the city and find the story and

04:45.640 --> 04:47.160
bring it back to us.

04:47.160 --> 04:49.080
And that's the next level and that's where we got to go.

04:49.080 --> 04:52.640
We got to think about how do we write apps that are algorithms that find stories, bring

04:52.640 --> 04:57.320
them and then we have a hundred of those out there sweeping the city while we sleep.

04:57.320 --> 05:02.760
Okay, now, or while we drink bourbon, right?

05:02.760 --> 05:08.720
So, okay, now the real reason I gave this talk, the real reason I gave this talk is

05:08.720 --> 05:09.720
to flip the script.

05:09.720 --> 05:13.800
There's five things that you geeks need to learn from reporters so that you can be better

05:13.800 --> 05:15.500
at what you do, right?

05:15.500 --> 05:17.680
Number one, trust no one.

05:17.680 --> 05:19.200
Don't trust your stupid data.

05:19.200 --> 05:23.640
This is a hilarious goddamn map that somebody made at Los Angeles who I will not name that

05:23.640 --> 05:28.200
claims that it is crime hotspots but because the person making it didn't have a freaking

05:28.200 --> 05:34.680
clue what they were putting in, Santa Monica is the reddest place on the goddamn map.

05:34.680 --> 05:38.520
That's on a major website right now, right?

05:38.520 --> 05:41.000
Number two, pick up the goddamn phone.

05:41.000 --> 05:42.600
Make a phone call.

05:42.600 --> 05:44.400
Find out what's going outside the office.

05:44.400 --> 05:48.040
Don't expect all these other people to bring data to you on a silver platter and have it

05:48.040 --> 05:49.280
just work perfectly.

05:49.280 --> 05:50.280
That's on you.

05:50.280 --> 05:51.680
You got to make it work.

05:51.680 --> 05:53.880
All right, number three, seek a rabbi.

05:53.880 --> 05:57.160
When you're on the phone, you got to find the people in the system who make the data,

05:57.160 --> 06:00.840
who know the data and nobody ever asked them how it works and when you do, they're going

06:00.840 --> 06:04.120
to love you and they're going to tell you everything about it and you're going to come

06:04.120 --> 06:08.400
out with a better story that nobody else has that really gets to the heart of it, right?

06:08.400 --> 06:11.720
Number four, once you have that story, just take it and run.

06:11.720 --> 06:15.360
Don't spend the next six months saying, oh, I need an app that does everything and has

06:15.360 --> 06:17.440
all these magic features and runs forever.

06:17.440 --> 06:19.960
If you've got a story, run, tell that, right?

06:19.960 --> 06:20.960
You don't need to sit and wait.

06:20.960 --> 06:21.960
You need to pivot.

06:21.960 --> 06:24.280
You need to cut and you need to take it to the end zone.

06:24.280 --> 06:26.760
And that's why I mean I say finish.

06:26.760 --> 06:30.200
There is nothing a geek likes more than starting a new project.

06:30.200 --> 06:35.640
Am I right?

